WEBVTT

00:00:00.173 --> 00:00:04.355
{happy}Hello, and a very warm welcome to Shah Spectrum.

00:00:04.355 --> 00:00:05.204
If you're new here, welcome.

00:00:05.204 --> 00:00:09.720
If you're back again, it's lovely to have you with us once more.

00:00:10.318 --> 00:00:14.544
Nobody in America is voting for a president this time.

00:00:14.544 --> 00:00:20.481
So why are bond markets, central banks and finance ministries around the world

00:00:20.481 --> 00:00:21.545
watching so closely?

00:00:21.545 --> 00:00:24.738
Because this vote decides who controls the country's purse strings.

00:00:25.835 --> 00:00:27.573
I'm Mittal Shah.

00:00:27.573 --> 00:00:29.529
Let's unpack what is really at stake.

00:00:29.529 --> 00:00:32.658
{fluent}The United States is holding its midterm elections.

00:00:32.658 --> 00:00:37.960
They are called midterms because they fall in the middle of a president's four-year

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:38.560
term.

00:00:38.191 --> 00:00:43.954
All four hundred and thirty-five seats in the House of Representatives are up for

00:00:43.954 --> 00:00:44.646
election.

00:00:44.646 --> 00:00:48.565
So are thirty-five of the hundred seats in the Senate.

00:00:48.565 --> 00:00:54.278
Together, the House and the Senate make up Congress, the body that writes American

00:00:54.278 --> 00:00:54.878
law.

00:00:55.143 --> 00:00:57.297
Here is the pattern to know.

00:00:57.297 --> 00:01:01.495
Since 1938, the president's party has lost House seats in twenty of twenty-two

00:01:01.495 --> 00:01:02.402
midterm elections.

00:01:02.402 --> 00:01:04.993
In 2010, Barack Obama's Democrats lost sixty-three seats.

00:01:04.993 --> 00:01:09.024
In 2018, Donald Trump's Republicans lost forty-one.

00:01:09.024 --> 00:01:14.025
As of the end of October, forecasters expect the Democrats to win the House by a

00:01:14.025 --> 00:01:15.644
wide margin.

00:01:15.644 --> 00:01:19.492
Some give them the edge in the Senate too, but that looks closer.

00:01:19.492 --> 00:01:22.546
Remember, these are forecasts, not results.

00:01:23.096 --> 00:01:25.679
Why does any of this matter for the economy?

00:01:25.679 --> 00:01:30.289
Because of one rule in the American constitution:

00:01:30.289 --> 00:01:31.328
Congress holds the purse.

00:01:31.328 --> 00:01:33.234
It decides how much the government spends.

00:01:33.234 --> 00:01:38.501
It decides how much the government can borrow, through a cap called the debt limit.

00:01:38.501 --> 00:01:44.656
And the constitution also gives Congress the power to set tariffs, which are taxes

00:01:44.656 --> 00:01:45.846
on imported goods.

00:01:45.846 --> 00:01:51.463
The debt limit matters because, if it is not raised in time, the government could

00:01:51.463 --> 00:01:54.895
run short of money to pay bills it has already promised.

00:01:54.895 --> 00:01:58.114
That is why lenders and governments abroad pay attention.

00:01:58.691 --> 00:02:00.622
There is a recent example.

00:02:00.622 --> 00:02:05.535
In February, the Supreme Court ruled, by six votes to three, that the president's

00:02:05.535 --> 00:02:08.968
emergency powers law does not let him impose tariffs.

00:02:08.968 --> 00:02:14.396
The administration then turned to a different law, which allows a temporary tariff

00:02:14.396 --> 00:02:20.168
for at most one hundred and fifty days, unless Congress agrees to extend it.

00:02:20.168 --> 00:02:23.377
Tariffs have since stayed in place under other laws.

00:02:23.377 --> 00:02:26.243
But the episode shows why Congress matters.

00:02:26.739 --> 00:02:28.421
So what changes if the Democrats win the House?

00:02:28.421 --> 00:02:30.324
Probably not much overnight.

00:02:30.324 --> 00:02:33.894
A bill needs both chambers and the president's signature.

00:02:33.894 --> 00:02:39.300
A president can veto a bill, and overriding a veto takes two thirds of both

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:39.900
chambers.

00:02:39.764 --> 00:02:44.172
So a Democratic House could not simply repeal tariffs on its own.

00:02:44.172 --> 00:02:48.117
What it could do is refuse to pass the president's spending plans.

00:02:48.117 --> 00:02:52.860
Any bill that raises revenue, such as a tax, must start in the House, so that

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:54.593
chamber has real leverage.

00:02:55.175 --> 00:02:57.839
That brings us to the word analysts keep using:

00:02:57.839 --> 00:02:58.439
gridlock.

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:03.915
When one party holds the White House and the other holds a chamber, it is called

00:03:03.915 --> 00:03:05.086
divided government.

00:03:05.086 --> 00:03:07.038
Gridlock becomes more likely.

00:03:07.038 --> 00:03:11.196
Analysts say that raises the risk of a government shutdown, when funding runs out

00:03:11.196 --> 00:03:12.953
and parts of the government stop work.

00:03:12.953 --> 00:03:20.029
It also raises the risk of a fight over the debt limit, and those talks are expected

00:03:20.029 --> 00:03:21.607
in twenty twenty-seven.

00:03:22.213 --> 00:03:28.435
If one party wins everything, policy usually moves faster, whichever party that is.

00:03:28.435 --> 00:03:32.560
Divided government usually means slower, with more bargaining.

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:34.401
Neither is good or bad on its own.

00:03:34.401 --> 00:03:37.878
It is simply a different way for decisions to get made.

00:03:38.379 --> 00:03:39.215
What do we not know?

00:03:39.215 --> 00:03:39.815
Who wins.

00:03:39.627 --> 00:03:44.429
Close races can take days to call, so the picture may stay unclear for a while.

00:03:44.429 --> 00:03:50.337
And the effect on the economy depends less on who wins than on what Congress then

00:03:50.337 --> 00:03:51.915
actually passes, or fails to pass.

00:03:51.915 --> 00:03:53.041
{happy}To sum up:

00:03:53.041 --> 00:03:56.440
this election is not about who sits in the White House.

00:03:56.440 --> 00:04:01.008
It decides who controls the spending, the borrowing and the tariff rules, and

00:04:01.008 --> 00:04:04.613
whether those fights end in agreement or in gridlock.

00:04:04.613 --> 00:04:07.810
Thanks for listening, and see you in the next episode!

